---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import photos from '../data/photos.json';
const p=(id,w=1500)=>photos.find(x=>x.id===id).src+`?format=${w}w`;
const dimensions=['history','fun','food','trade','protection','carbon storage','health','culture'];
---
<BaseLayout title="A Sea of Fading Color | Bing Lin — University of Chicago Press" description="A Sea of Fading Color, a forthcoming 2028 University of Chicago Press book by Bing Lin, blends memoir, reporting, science and original photography to explore coral reefs." theme="dark" image={p('reef-wall',1500)}>
  <section class="book-opener"><img src={p('reef-wall',2500)} alt="Underwater coral reef photographed by Bing Lin" fetchpriority="high"/><div class="book-opener-tint"></div><div class="book-opener-content"><span class="overline">A BOOK BY BING LIN / COMING 2028</span><h1>A Sea<br />of <em>Fading</em><br />Color<span>.</span></h1><p>The Ebbing Life of Coral Reefs and Their Essential Place in Ours</p><div class="book-byline"><span>FORTHCOMING FROM THE UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO PRESS</span><a href="#about-the-book">ENTER THE BOOK ↓</a></div></div></section>
  <section class="book-premise" id="about-the-book"><div class="book-premise-number">01 / A LOVE LETTER AND A FIELD REPORT</div><div data-reveal><h2>What disappears when a reef <em>loses its color?</em></h2><p class="book-teaser">A deeply reported, visually rich journey into the disappearing world of coral reefs—and why their survival matters to us all.</p><p>I grew up in Indonesia, at the heart of the Coral Triangle, where I fell in love with the sea before I ever understood what a coral reef was. I became a Divemaster before I became a scientist, and I’ve watched the underwater world shift beneath my fins—from dazzling abundance to bleached and broken graveyards.</p><p><em>A Sea of Fading Color</em> is the book I’ve always wanted to write. It blends science, story, and lived experience—a field report from the front lines of coral reef collapse, and a love letter to what we still have left.</p></div></section>
  <section class="book-picture-essay" aria-label="Photographs from Bing Lin's reef portfolio"><figure><img src={p('turtle',1700)} alt="Sea turtle gliding over a coral reef" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>THE WORLDS BENEATH OUR FINS / © BING LIN</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={p('pink-corals',1300)} alt="Tiny reef fish among pink soft corals" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>AN ENTIRE WORLD IN A HANDFUL OF COLOR</figcaption></figure></section>
  <section class="book-eight"><div><span class="overline dark">02 / EIGHT DIMENSIONS OF OUR CONNECTION</span><h2>More than a beautiful<br /><em>place to visit.</em></h2><p>The book traces the invisible footprint coral reefs leave on our lives across eight life-sustaining dimensions.</p></div><ol>{dimensions.map((d,i)=><li><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span>{d}</li>)}</ol></section>
  <section class="book-letter"><div class="book-letter-image"><img src={p('shark',1700)} alt="Shark passing beneath a coral ledge" loading="lazy"/></div><div class="book-letter-text" data-reveal><span class="overline">03 / FROM THE FIELD</span><h2>Wonder.<br />Loss.<br /><em>Resilience.</em></h2><p>This story is grounded in fieldwork and enriched with a full-color insert of original photography—capturing moments of wonder, loss, and resilience from some of the world’s most biodiverse and at-risk seascapes.</p><p>I write about bleached corals and marine heatwaves, about communities navigating change, about fishers, tourists, scientists, and the tangled human relationships that shape the fate of our oceans.</p><p>Part memoir, part investigative reporting, and part field guide to reef conservation in the Anthropocene, <em>A Sea of Fading Color</em> invites you to see coral reefs not just as beautiful places to visit, but as living ecosystems worth fighting for.</p></div></section>
  <section class="book-colophon"><div><p class="overline dark">THE BOOK / IN BRIEF</p><h2>Still <em>in the making.</em></h2></div><div><dl><div><dt>TITLE</dt><dd>A Sea of Fading Color: The Ebbing Life of Coral Reefs and Their Essential Place in Ours</dd></div><div><dt>AUTHOR</dt><dd>Bing Lin</dd></div><div><dt>PUBLISHER</dt><dd><a href="https://press.uchicago.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Chicago Press ↗</a></dd></div><div><dt>EXPECTED PUBLICATION</dt><dd>2028</dd></div><div><dt>SUPPORT</dt><dd>Alfred P. Sloan Foundation book grant (2026–2028)</dd></div></dl><p>The book is under contract. Publication details and cover artwork may change before release.</p><a class="text-arrow" href="mailto:thebinglin@gmail.com?subject=A%20Sea%20of%20Fading%20Color">INQUIRE ABOUT THE BOOK ↗</a></div></section>
  <section class="book-epilogue" style={`--book-last:url('${p('book-reef',2200)}')`}><div><span class="overline">A SEA OF FADING COLOR / 2028</span><h2>There is still<br /><em>so much to save.</em></h2><a href="/photography/">EXPLORE THE PHOTOGRAPHY ↗</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
